
#import "../../../_lib/kodama.typ": *

#show: kodama

#metadata((
  title: "Perfectoid",
))

#subtree(
  title: "perfectoid ring",
  taxon: "definition"
)[
  A Tate ring $R$ is perfectoid if it is complete uniform and there exists a pseudo-uniformizer $pi.alt in R$ such that $p in pi.alt^p R^(circle)$ and the Frobenius map $Phi : R^(circle) slash p -> R^(circle) slash p$ is surjective.
]

The Frobenius map
$
  Phi : R^(circle) slash pi.alt -> R^(circle) slash pi.alt^p
$
is actually an isomorphism for any such pseudo-uniformizer $pi.alt$, as $R^(circle)$ is integrally closed.

#subtree(
  title: "tilting and untilting",
  taxon: "section"
)[
For a perfectoid Tate ring $R$, the tilt of $R$ is defined by the construction:
$
  R^flat & := lim_(x |-> x^p) R
$
with the inverse limit topology. For any $x := {x_n}_(n in NN) in R^flat$, let $x^sharp := x_0$. The topological commutative monoid $R^flat$ becomes a topological ring via:
$
  {x_n}_(n in NN) + {y_n}_(n in NN) := {lim_(m -> infinity)  (x_(n+m) + y_(n+m))^(p^m)}_(n in NN)
$

#subtree(
  taxon: "lemma"
)[
  The power-bounded elements $R^(flat circle) subset R^(circle)$ is:
  $
    R^(flat circle) = lim_(x |-> x^p) R^(circle) = lim_(x |-> x^p) R^(circle) slash pi.alt 
  $
  Furthermore, there exists $pi.alt^(flat) in R^(flat circle)$ such that 
  $
    pi.alt^(flat sharp) & = pi.alt \ 
    R^(flat) & = R^(flat circle)[(pi.alt^flat)^(-1)] \
    R^(flat circle) slash pi.alt^flat & = R^(circle) slash pi.alt
  $
]

#subtree(
  taxon: "lemma"
)[
  The set of open integrally closed subring $R^(flat+) subset R^(flat)$ is in bijection with the set of open integrally closed subring $R^+ subset R$.
]

#proof()[
  An open integrally closed subring can be regarded as integrally closed subring of $R^(circle)$ containing $R^(circle circle)$. The quotient $R^(circle) slash R^(circle circle)$ is fixed under tilting.
]

#subtree(
  taxon: "theorem"
)[
  If $(R, R^+)$ is perfectoid, there exists a surjection $theta$:
  $
    W(R^(flat+)) & -> R^+ \
    sum_(n>=0) [r_n] p^n & |-> sum_(n>=0) r_n^sharp p^n
  $
  whose kernel is generated by a non-zero divisor $xi = p + [pi.alt^flat] alpha$ for the pseudo-uniformizer $pi.alt^flat in R^(flat+)$ and $alpha in W(R^(flat+))$. 
]

#proof()[
  We show that the map:
  $
    W(R^+) & -> R^+ slash pi.alt^m \
    {x_n}_(n>=0) & |-> sum_(n=0)^(m) x_n^(p^(m-n)) p^n
  $
  factors through $W(R^+ slash pi.alt) = W(R^(flat +) slash pi.alt^flat)$. The desired map $theta$ equals to the limit of 
  $
    W(R^(flat+)) -> W(R^(flat+) slash pi.alt^(flat)) -> R^+
  $
  Note that $W(R^(flat+))$ is $[pi.alt^flat]$-adically complete, $R^+$ is $pi.alt$-adically complete and $theta([pi.alt^flat]) = pi.alt$. The surjectivity of $theta$ follows from the surjectivity of $W(R^(flat+)) -> R^+ slash pi.alt$.

  By surjectivity of $theta$, we write:
  $
    p slash pi.alt = sum_(n>=0) r_n^sharp p^n in R^+
  $
  We now define $xi = p - [pi.alt^flat] (sum_(n>=0) [r_n] p^n) in ker theta$. Then:
  - $W(R^(flat+))$ is $[pi.alt^flat]$-adically complete and $[pi.alt^flat]$-torsion free
  - $W(R^(flat+)) slash [pi.alt^flat] = W(R^(flat+) slash [pi.alt^flat])$ is $xi$-adically complete and $xi$-torsion free
  As a consequence of commutative algebra, $W(R^(flat+))$ is $xi$-adically complete and $xi$-torsion free. The quotient $W(R^(flat+)) slash xi$ is $[pi.alt^flat]$-adically complete and $[pi.alt^flat]$-torsion free. The isomorphism now follows from $W(R^(flat+)) slash ([pi.alt^flat], xi) = R^(flat+) slash pi.alt^flat = R^+ slash pi.alt$.
]

#subtree(
  taxon: "corollary"
)[
  If $R$ is perfectoid with tilt $R^flat$, then tilting induces an equivalence between the category of perfectoid $R$-algebras and the category of perfectoid $R^flat$-algebras. The inverse functor is given by:
  $
    A |-> (W(A^(circle)) slash xi)[pi.alt^(-1)]
  $
]


]

/*
#subtree(
  title: "Perfectoid rings",
  taxon: "section"
)[
Recall the $p$-Witt ring $W_p$ defined in #local("../../Prismatic/Witt.typst").

#subtree(
  title: "perfectoid ring",
  taxon: "definition"
)[
  A Tate ring $R$ is perfectoid if it is complete uniform and there exists a pseudo-uniformizer $pi.alt in R$ such that $p in pi.alt^p R^(circle)$ and the Frobenius map $Phi : R^(circle) slash pi.alt -> R^(circle) slash pi.alt^p$ is isomorphic.
]

#subtree(
  taxon: "remark"
)[
  For any pseudo-uniformizer $pi.alt$, the Frobenius map $Phi : R^(circle) slash pi.alt -> R^(circle) slash pi.alt^p$ is isomorphic if and only if $Phi : R slash p -> R slash p$ is surjective.
  - $Phi$ is automatically injective since if $x^p in pi.alt^p R^(circle)$, then $(pi.alt^(-1) x)^p in R^(circle)$. In particular, $x in pi.alt R^(circle)$ as $R^(circle)$ is integrally closed.
  - If $Phi : R^(circle) slash pi.alt -> R^(circle) slash pi.alt^p$ is surjective, then by $pi.alt^p$ adic approximation:
    $
      x = sum_(i >= 0) x_i^(p) pi.alt^(i p) = (sum_(i >= 0) x_i pi.alt^i)^p mod p
    $
]

#subtree(
  taxon: "proposition"
)[
  If $R$ is defined over $FF_p$, then $R$ is perfectoid Tate ring if and only if $R$ is perfect complete Tate ring.
]

#subtree(
  taxon: "proposition"
)[
  Let $K$ be a nonarchimedean field, then $K$ is a perfectoid field if and only if:
  - $K$ is not discretely valued.
  - the absolute value $abs(p) < 1$.
  - the Frobenius $Phi : cal(O)_K slash p -> cal(O)_K slash p$ is surjective.
]

#subtree(
  taxon: "proposition"
)[
  A rational subset of a perfectoid ring is still perfectoid. In particular, a perfectoid Tate ring is stably uniform and hence sheafy. 
]

#subtree(
  title: "tilting",
  taxon: "definition"
)[
  titling
  // Let $R$ be a perfectoid Tate ring, the tilt of $R$ is defined by:
  // $
  //   R^flat := lim_(x |-> x^p) R
  // $
  // for an integrally closed subring $R^+ subset R$, one also defines:
  // $
  //   R^(flat+) := lim_(x |-> x^p) R^+
  // $
  // We write $(-)^sharp$ for the projection $R^flat -> R$. $R^flat$ is equipped with a perfectoid $FF_p$-algebra structure extending the commutative monoid structure:
  // $
  //   (x+y)^((i)) = lim_(n -> infinity) (x^((i+n)) + y^((i+n)))^(p^n)
  // $
  // We have $R^flat = R^(flat +)[(pi.alt^(flat))^(-1)]$ where $pi.alt^(flat sharp) = pi.alt$. Then $op("Spa")(R, R^+) = op("Spa")(R^flat, R^(flat+))$ preserving rational subsets. There exists a surjective ring homomorphism:
  // $
  //   W(R^(flat+)) & -> R^+ \
  //   sum_(n>=0) [r_n] p^n & |-> sum_(n>=0) r_n^sharp p^n
  // $
  // The kernel is generated by a non-zero divisor $xi = p + [pi.alt^flat] alpha$ for a pseudo-uniformizer $pi.alt^flat in R^(flat+)$ and $alpha in W(R^(flat+))$. Tilting and untilting defines equivalence:
  // - between the category of $(S, S^+)$ perfectoid Tate ring $S$ and an open integrally closed subring $S^+$ and the category of $(R, R^+, I)$ a perfectoid Tate ring $R$ of characteristic $p$, an open integrally closed subring $R^+$ and an ideal $I$ which is primitive of degree $1$.
  // - between the category of perfectoid $R$-algebras and perfectoid $R^flat$-algebras.
]
]
*/

